Z||000003||Ditz88GT||06-03-2000||10:18 PM||||No, it a round metal piece that sort of looks like a big round relay.  Well I guess it is some sort of a relay.  It's the part that turns the power to the signals on and off.  Sould be under the dash.  I'm not real sure where, maybe somebody else can point you exactly to it.  I'm just not sure where it's at in the Fieros.  I didn't have to replace one yet.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Brian||06-04-2000||07:23 AM||||playazi<P>Do you mean that both sides don't work?<BR>If it's just the one side check the front bulb & the sockets ground.<P>The flasher is located under the passenger side dash. Look for a blue box about the size of a pack of smokes. The "flasher" is plugged in right beside this.            ||||reg||1
Z||000005||iplayazi||06-04-2000||12:33 PM||||Ive replaced the round fuse and both sides dont work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">( just comes on and stays on till I turn it off. One of my headlights blew. would that be it?? doubt it but any help is appreciated! ||||reg||9
Z||000006||iplayazi||06-04-2000||04:52 PM||||Man am I ever stuck. The hazards work fine, and all the orange lights are working.||||reg||5
Z||000007||redfiero||06-05-2000||07:02 AM||||Your turn signal socket is not grounded.<P>Quick test.<BR>Remove your front turn signals.  Remove the black wire on the turn signals.  Insert a wire in that socket and ground it to the car.  Now see if your signal blinks.  Let me know.<BR>matt||||reg||1
Z||000008||iplayazi||06-05-2000||08:42 PM||||You mean the handle, by the tilt selector.||||reg||1
Z||000009||redfiero||06-06-2000||06:56 AM||||No.  You need to remove the front turn signals from the car.  I think its (2) screws.  The problem is in your turn signal sockets (where the lights plug in).  Fieros are notorious for the ground plug breaking.||||reg||1
Z||000010||LarryB||06-06-2000||12:52 PM||||I just went through this exercise last night, the front assembly screws in using 2 Torx (T-15?) screws, you unscrew the assembly and the socket twists out.  The wiring isn't long enough to pull the socket out with a lot of slack wire so you need to feed it back in and up by the headlight. If you get the headlights up it will be a LOT easier to work on.  It's not difficult to snake the wire/socket back through afterward if the headlight is out of the way. <P>The local u-pull-it yard charged me $2.00 for a socket with a couple of inches of the 3 wires (blue/brown/black).  Either socket will work on either side, you can tell the side it originally came from, a light blue wire -&gt; left side, darker blue -&gt; right side, I just cut out the old one and spliced the new one in, voila, problem solved. Even under close inspection the old socket looked just fine. <P>In my case the turn signals worked but the left front markers wouldn't light if you put the headlights on, measuring voltage across the left front side marker showed only about 7 volts and the left turn signal on the dash would light up steady.  Even then the turn signals still worked properly.<P>From looking at my 22P(parts book) GM never sold the sockets by themselves, they were only available as part of a wiring harness assembly. <P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by redfiero:<BR><B>No.  You need to remove the front turn signals from the car.  I think its (2) screws.  The problem is in your turn signal sockets (where the lights plug in).  Fieros are notorious for the ground plug breaking.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 06-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||elfiero||06-06-2000||01:02 PM||||another thing to look at.check all the rear <BR>and side bulbs by hand inspect each to<BR>make sure the filiments are OK the way the<BR>system works,if you don't have enough<BR>current draw,they won't flash.||||reg||3
